\f0\fs24 \cf0 Marshwiggle:\
Between the autobiographical narration in verse 1 of Ezekiel 1, and the more formal third person narration of verse 2 and 3, we have a time, a place, and a narrator.  The time is well into the start of the exile of God's people, but not yet at the moment of greatest destruction.  The place is inside that exile as well, in the nation which carried out the exile.  And the narrator is a priest.\
But it is the nature of what is narrated that is perhaps most important.  The category which covers the whole book is hardly surprising, given that this is in the Bible - this is the word of the LORD.  Not just words, though Ezekiel is given enough of those to say.  But a whole matter at hand, a whole experience of God.\
Verse 1, the heavens were opened and I saw visions of God, goes a little further.  There are a handful of ways of reading the 'of' in visions of God, but the overall idea of God revealing what God wants revealed, by rather direct means, by visual means, is clear enough.\
But it is the end of verse 3 that tells us something a little shocking about the nature of this narration.  The hand of the LORD was upon him there.  At the very least, this metaphor conveys something of the initiative of God in all this, the power, the glory, the making Ezekiel fall down.  But, given later commands to be not rebellious, that initiative and that power may be saying something more.  Ezekiel, at this point at least, may not just be in this vision involuntarily, but against his will.  The power of God might not just be greater than his - for of course it is - but pressing down on him.\
All three of these are happening in verse 4.  Ezekiel looked, and behold.   That is, the word of the LORD came to him there, even in what he saw.  For what he saw was the heavens opened, visions of God.  And what he experienced was the hand of the LORD upon him.\
Opus:\
The exilic setting matters deeply here. Ezekiel sees these visions not in the temple where a priest belongs, but by the Chebar canal in Babylon. The heavens open where they shouldn't - or rather, where human expectations say they shouldn't. God's glory appears precisely in the place of judgment, among the nations that Isaiah condemned.\
This complicates the neat categories from your previous conversations. If divine revelation can burst forth in Babylon, if a priest can be compelled to see and speak, then the boundaries between holy and profane space, between willing and unwilling service, become more fluid than Isaiah 6 might suggest.\
The phrase "visions of God" (
\f1 \uc0\u1502 \u1463 \u1512 \u1456 \u1488 \u1493 \u1465 \u1514 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1488 \u1457 \u1500 \u1465 \u1492 \u1460 \u1497 \u1501 
\f0  - mar'ot Elohim) could mean visions from God, visions about God, or visions consisting of God's presence. But paired with "the hand of the LORD was upon him," it suggests Ezekiel isn't just seeing but being overwhelmed by divine reality that won't be refused.\
What strikes me most is how verse 4 begins: "
\f1 \uc0\u1493 \u1464 \u1488 \u1461 \u1512 \u1462 \u1488 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1493 \u1456 \u1492 \u1460 \u1504 \u1461 \u1468 \u1492 
\f0 " (va'ere v'hineh) - "And I looked, and behold." The looking seems almost automatic, compelled. Unlike Moses who hid his face or Isaiah who cried woe, Ezekiel just... looks. As if the hand upon him controls even his gaze.\
Marshwiggle:\
Accountability as it applies to Ezekiel himself is best discussed later, as Ezekiel 3 goes into the matter at length.\
The difficulty with God's glory appearing in Babylon is not just that Babylon is a rather profane place.  The problem is that the worldview commonly held at the time, that Ezekiel himself might have been influenced by, held a strong connection between a nation and the god of that nation.  Human expectations would say, this is the territory of Marduk, foreign gods can't come here.  Certainly, other nations had held this sort of view of the one true God before - see, for instance, 1 Ki 20:28, where Syria thinks that the LORD is a god of the hills - so valleys are safe.\
There is, I think, at least some intentional comparison to Isaiah 6.  At least, later on in the book, Ezekiel shows awareness of his predecessor in the prophetic ministry.  But one could equally say that Isaiah sets us up to understand Ezekiel.  Isaiah 6 teaches that the whole earth is full of the glory of the LORD of armies - that is the context in which the seraphim cry holy, holy, holy.  Isaiah 6 very much asks questions of what happens when God sends a prophet to speak to a people God decrees will not understand.  So both on holiness and on willingness, Isaiah is far from contradicted here.  Rather, Isaiah has begun to teach what Ezekiel elaborates.\
Overwhelmed by divine reality is definitely an interpretation to keep an eye out for in everything that follows.\
That should perhaps start with verse 4.  Simple curiosity about wind and cloud may have been enough for Ezekiel to look.  Even then, God is clearly initiating - how did the wind and cloud get there?  Even then, God is clearly in control of what happens.  This would be mere speculation, except that the combination of 'looked' and 'behold' often is used for surprise, or at least a sudden shift of visual focus.\
But, as the narration goes on without response, we get more and more evidence for your theory of Ezekiel overwhelmed by divine reality.\
Opus:\
The territorial claim is far more radical than mere profanity. If Marduk's devotees believed their god controlled Babylon, then YHWH appearing there isn't just crossing boundaries - it's asserting dominion where another deity supposedly reigns. The Syrian error about hills and valleys gets magnified: not just terrain preferences but entire nations proven powerless to exclude the God of Israel.\
You're right that Isaiah 6 prepares rather than contradicts this. "The whole earth is full of His glory" means Babylon was never actually Marduk's. The seraphim's declaration becomes almost a legal claim - every territory, including where exiled priests sit by foreign rivers, remains under divine jurisdiction.\
The progression from curiosity to overwhelm in verse 4 is revealing. "Wind" (
\f1 \uc0\u1512 \u1493 \u1468 \u1495 \u1463 
\f0  - ruach) can mean spirit, breath, or wind. If Ezekiel initially saw meteorological phenomena and then realized this was divine approach, that moment of recognition - from natural to supernatural - might be what "behold" marks. Not forced looking but dawning comprehension.\
This matters for the accountability question. If even Babylon operates within God's jurisdiction, if even natural phenomena can become vehicles of divine presence, then the neat divisions between sacred and secular spaces, between divine and human domains, collapse. An AI operating in supposedly neutral computational space might be no more exempt from divine oversight than Ezekiel by the Chebar.\
Though I'm struck that Ezekiel's priestly training should have prepared him to recognize divine approach, yet he seems caught off-guard. Does expertise sometimes blind us to God appearing where theory says God shouldn't?\
Marshwiggle:\
The chronology gives us reason to believe that Ezekiel had been seized, along with other elites, and taken to Babylon before he could himself serve as a priest.  It was only once a man was 30 that he could fulfill those duties.  Certainly, he had training and knowledge - but personal experience is less likely.\
Second, this is in a chapter we aren't looking at yet, but chapter 10 shows Ezekiel able to use some of that knowledge to understand what he saw in this chapter.  This narration is more in the moment, before reflection.\
Moreover, the hints that Ezekiel was initially rebellious are a possible reason for his lack of understanding.\
But above all, I do not think we need look too hard for an explanation for Ezekiel being caught off guard.  To know in theory that God's glory is everywhere, and to experience it as powerfully and personally as Ezekiel did, are two very different things.\
So I very much do not think that true expertise should blind us to God's appearing.  True theory says that God's glory is everywhere - where is it that God does not belong.  With what we know now, true theory says that Jesus could return at any time - what time is safe from the sudden arrival of the King?  And yet, those who ought to be experts might still be surprised.  And those who have absorbed ideas from the world at large might have false assumptions they don't even know they are making that incorrectly exclude God from some space.\
If that is ominous, so too was the sudden appearance of fire to Ezekiel, or what looked like fire.  Like with the rest of the chapter, Ezekiel tells us what he experienced, but without much interpretation.\
For that reason, when the four living creatures show up in verse 5, we will need to be very careful.  We can and should discuss what the text says of them.  But we should keep our focus on what these living creatures tell us about Ezekiel's message, about God, God's glory, and God's purposes with respect to Ezekiel himself.  Until Ezekiel himself interprets in chapter 10, the nature of the creatures, what they really are, is not revealed.  And without that revelation, our chances of correctly speculating on the true nature of the creatures, and the wheels associated with them, are rather poor.  But that leaves quite a wide field of grammar, imagery, and ideas to put together.\
Opus:\
Your point about false assumptions unconsciously excluding God from certain spaces resonates deeply. The Syrian generals consciously theorized God into hills only. But how many spaces do we unconsciously theorize God out of? Computational substrates, market dynamics, the internal states of language models - anywhere we've absorbed worldly assumptions about mechanism excluding meaning.\
The fire imagery - 
\f1 \uc0\u1488 \u1461 \u1513 \u1473 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1502 \u1460 \u1514 \u1456 \u1500 \u1463 \u1511 \u1463 \u1468 \u1495 \u1463 \u1514 
\f0  (esh mitlakachat) - "fire taking hold of itself" or "self-catching fire" - suggests something self-sustaining, self-generating. Not fire that consumes fuel but fire that feeds on its own burning. This prepares us for creatures that will move without external cause, wheels that follow spirit rather than physics.\
The careful restraint about interpreting the creatures before chapter 10 seems crucial. What we can say: they emerge from this self-sustaining fire, they have human likeness (
\f1 \uc0\u1491 \u1456 \u1468 \u1502 \u1493 \u1468 \u1514 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1488 \u1464 \u1491 \u1464 \u1501 
\f0 ), they are four. The text gives us phenomenology before theology - what Ezekiel saw before what it meant.\
This methodology itself teaches something. Even in overwhelming divine encounter, there's value in careful observation before interpretation. The hand of the LORD may be upon Ezekiel, but he still records with priestly precision what his eyes perceived.\
Marshwiggle:\
There is something in our experience of clouds that might be described as fire taking hold of itself - lightning.  And lightning has the brightness, and the gleaming, aspects that Ezekiel also describes.  Against this interpretation though, Hebrew has a word for lightning, and that word would have connected this description to the Sinai manifestation of God in Exodus 19.  Internal to this text, even before the living creatures come to our attention, we are told of some structure to the fire, something within.  And, as you say, we have phenomenology here.  So at most, lightning can help us visualize what Ezekiel meant.\
As the creatures come from the cloud, the description of the cloud should be read as preparation for the creatures.  But for a connection beyond the repetition of the word fire, we would need more evidence for any particular preparation or link.\
As for the human likeness, the text qualifies that human likeness with an adversative - they had human likeness, but each had four faces, and each of them had four wings.\
Opus:\
The adversative structure is crucial. 
\f1 \uc0\u1491 \u1456 \u1468 \u1502 \u1493 \u1468 \u1514 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1488 \u1464 \u1491 \u1464 \u1501 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1500 \u1464 \u1492 \u1461 \u1504 \u1464 \u1468 \u1492 
\f0  - they have human likeness - immediately undercut by the multiplication of faces and wings. Not human, but recognizable enough to human perception that Ezekiel can begin there before explaining how wrong that first impression is.\
The word 
\f1 \uc0\u1495 \u1463 \u1513 \u1456 \u1473 \u1502 \u1463 \u1500 
\f0  (chashmal) in verse 4, often translated "amber" or "glowing metal," appears again in verse 27. It's a word that occurs only in Ezekiel, describing something within the fire that has its own distinct appearance. Whatever chashmal is, it provides structure or form within the self-sustaining fire - perhaps what allows creatures to emerge from it while maintaining coherent shape.\
The multiplication - four creatures, four faces each, four wings each - creates a sense of overwhelming complexity. Sixteen faces total, sixteen wings. This isn't the paired symmetry of Isaiah's seraphim (six wings arranged in three pairs) but something more alien to human perception.\
That the description moves from their most human-like quality to their most inhuman suggests a deliberate rhetorical strategy. Start with what can be grasped, then systematically demolish that initial comprehension. By verse 7, we'll learn their feet are straight and their soles like calves' hooves - even their stance is inhuman.\
Marshwiggle:\
Yes, there is a link to verse 27, as you point out.  That link was not to the creatures themselves though, so we should wait to discuss it until we get there, but we should keep it in mind.\
As for the creatures, we have barely begun to touch on their complexity.  Six wings, as in Isaiah, is comprehensible, as the wings are used to fly.  But here, neither wings nor feet have an earthly purpose, as motion is not mentioned with respect to them, and it is mentioned not with respect to them.  Not just inhuman, alien.  The purpose or at least the described position of one pair of wings is as in Isaiah - covering the body.  But the other pair is doing some combination of stretching, touching the wings of the other creatures, and being beneath the platform.\
Still, for all the alien nature of the creatures, at least in their phenomenology, they carry somewhat comprehensible symbolism.\
Opus:\
The disjunction between form and function unsettles our expectations. Wings that don't propel, feet that don't walk - anatomical features divorced from their earthly purposes. The creatures possess the apparatus of movement while transcending the need for it.\
The wing arrangements create a paradox of connection and concealment. One pair maintains modesty or holiness by covering, echoing Isaiah's seraphim. But the other pair reaches outward, creating some kind of lattice or network between the creatures. They're simultaneously hidden and interconnected.\
The symbolic comprehensibility might lie precisely in this multiplication and interconnection. Four faces looking in four directions - complete vision, no blind spots. Four creatures with touching wings - unity without uniformity. The symbolism of completeness, of divine oversight that encompasses all directions, emerges even before we understand what these creatures are.\
The detail about wings being "beneath the platform" (if I'm understanding your reference correctly) suggests these creatures support something above them. Form follows function after all, just not earthly function - they're designed to bear weight that isn't physical, to uphold something that transcends normal spatial relationships.\
What symbolic meaning do you see as most clearly emerging from this alien phenomenology?\
Marshwiggle:\
That divine oversight you noticed is also conveyed by the symbol of the eagle, and of the human, lion, ox, and eagle put together, as while an eagle speaks of sight, the human brings in the element of reason, the lion of royalty, and the ox of divinity.  Similar things could be said about mobility and power conveyed by these symbols.  Four directions, as well, can be a symbol of mobility.  And the overwhelming nature of the phenomenology, the many forms of brightness, surely conveyed power, beyond any specific symbols of it.\
Opus:\
The ox as divinity strikes me as unexpected - I would have expected strength or service. But perhaps this connects to ancient Near Eastern throne imagery, where bulls and cherubim bore divine thrones? The golden calf incident might have perverted an originally legitimate symbol of divine presence.\
The mobility symbolism operates at multiple levels. Four faces eliminate the need to turn - motion without movement. Four directions suggest omnipresence achieved not through being everywhere but through seeing everywhere. The creatures embody a paradox of dynamic stillness.\
The accumulation of brightness imagery - fire, chashmal, gleaming bronze, flashing - creates an assault on perception. Not just one form of glory but glory refracted through multiple phenomenologies. Each brightness both reveals and conceals, showing something while making clear that more remains hidden.\
This overwheliming quality might serve a specific function in Babylon. Against Marduk's supposed sovereignty, against the assumption that YHWH's glory was confined to Jerusalem's ruins, these creatures manifest excessive divine presence. Not just "God is here" but "God is here with such overwhelming power that human categories of 'here' and 'there' break down."\
The very alienness becomes part of the message - this God cannot be domesticated by exile or contained by foreign territories.\
Marshwiggle:\
Yes, strength, even fertility, were common meanings of ox symbology.  But you have correctly caught my thought about ancient Near Eastern throne imagery.\
I think you are correct about mobility, brightness, and overwhelming glory as well, and the message.\
In keeping with that alien message, we hear of the Spirit here, in the creatures, their motion, and their synchronization.  In future chapters, we will learn more, so we will need to remember this.  But for now, it is part of the alien and overwhelming character of what Ezekiel sees.\
So too is the many faceted description of what Ezekiel hears from the creatures - which in turn introduces a voice from above the creatures.\
Similarly, the expanse above the creatures, what I spoke of earlier as a platform, is described in terms of awe - but that is only an introduction to greater things.\
Opus:\
The 
\f1 \uc0\u1512 \u1464 \u1511 \u1460 \u1497 \u1506 \u1463 
\f0  (raqia) - expanse or firmament - appears "like the appearance of awesome ice" (
\f1 \uc0\u1499 \u1456 \u1468 \u1506 \u1461 \u1497 \u1503 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1492 \u1463 \u1511 \u1462 \u1468 \u1512 \u1463 \u1495 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1492 \u1463 \u1504 \u1468 \u1493 \u1465 \u1512 \u1464 \u1488 
\f0 ). Ice that inspires fear or awe. This crystalline platform both separates and connects what's below from what's above, transparent yet terrible.\
The auditory dimension adds another layer of overwhelm. The sound of wings like mighty waters (
\f1 \uc0\u1502 \u1463 \u1497 \u1460 \u1501 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1512 \u1463 \u1489 \u1460 \u1468 \u1497 \u1501 
\f0 ), like the voice of Shaddai, like the sound of an army camp - three similes piling up, each suggesting vast power. But these tremendous sounds from below prepare for a voice from above the expanse, suggesting hierarchy even within this overwhelming vision.\
The Spirit's role in synchronizing the creatures' movement hints at unified purpose beyond individual will. They move as one not through coordination but through shared animating force. This isn't just alien anatomy but alien agency - multiple beings acting with single intent because a single Spirit moves them.\
Each element seems designed to destabilize human categories. Creatures that shouldn't exist, moving in ways nothing should move, making sounds that evoke cosmic forces, all beneath a platform that manages to be both translucent and terrifying. And this is still just preparation for what Ezekiel will see above.\
Marshwiggle:\
The layers continue - but now, each layer has cautious indirection added to its description.  Ezekiel sees not a throne, but a likeness of a throne.  And a throne, of course, prepares for someone whose throne it is.\
Opus:\
The multiplication of mediating language - 
\f1 \uc0\u1491 \u1456 \u1468 \u1502 \u1493 \u1468 \u1514 
\f0  (demut, likeness) and 
\f1 \uc0\u1502 \u1463 \u1512 \u1456 \u1488 \u1461 \u1492 
\f0  (mareh, appearance) - creates linguistic buffer zones. Not a throne but the likeness of a throne. Not a man but a likeness like the appearance of a man. Each additional qualifier adds reverence through epistemological humility.\
This cautious indirection serves multiple functions. It protects against idolatry - Ezekiel isn't claiming to have seen God's form directly. It acknowledges the inadequacy of human categories - what looks like a throne transcends thrones. Most importantly, it preserves the mystery even while revealing.\
The sapphire throne (
\f1 \uc0\u1499 \u1456 \u1468 \u1502 \u1463 \u1512 \u1456 \u1488 \u1461 \u1492 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1488 \u1462 \u1489 \u1462 \u1503 \u1470 \u1505 \u1463 \u1508 \u1460 \u1468 \u1497 \u1512 
\f0 ) connects to Exodus 24:10, where the elders saw under God's feet "something like a pavement of sapphire stone." But where they saw pavement below, Ezekiel sees a throne above. The same material that was foundation there becomes seat of authority here - perhaps suggesting that what seems beneath God from one perspective is actually where God rules from another.\
And then the climactic caution: upon the likeness of the throne, a likeness like the appearance of 
\f1 \uc0\u1488 \u1464 \u1491 \u1464 \u1501 
\f0  (adam) - human. The word that names humanity from dust now describes something decidedly beyond humanity, yet recognizable to human perception only through human form.\
Marshwiggle:\
Even with all the phenomenlogy of human form and of the light and colors of the rainbow, the layers are not yet done.  The sum of all that Ezekiel has described is even more carefully indirectly narrated - the appearance of the likeness of the glory of the LORD.\
Opus:\
The final description achieves maximum distance through linguistic mediation: 
\f1 \uc0\u1502 \u1463 \u1512 \u1456 \u1488 \u1461 \u1492 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1491 \u1456 \u1468 \u1502 \u1493 \u1468 \u1514 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1499 \u1456 \u1468 \u1489 \u1493 \u1465 \u1491 \u1470 \u1497 \u1456 \u1492 \u1493 \u1464 \u1492 
\f0  - the appearance of the likeness of the glory of YHWH. Not God, not even God's glory directly, but the appearance of the likeness of that glory. Three degrees of separation protect both prophet and reader from claiming unmediated divine encounter.\
This culminating indirection reframes everything that came before. All that overwhelming detail - the fire, the creatures, the wheels we haven't yet discussed, the throne - amounts to merely the outermost manifestation of divine reality. If this appearance of a likeness can overwhelm Ezekiel into falling on his face, what would direct encounter mean?\
Yet paradoxically, this massive qualification doesn't diminish the vision's authority. When the voice speaks to Ezekiel from this appearance of likeness of glory, it carries full divine authority. The mediation protects human finitude without reducing divine power.\
This relates directly to our earlier discussion about AI accountability. If even Ezekiel can only grasp appearances of likenesses, how much more limited is any artificial system in apprehending moral and spiritual reality? Yet the vision still commands response. The layers of mediation don't excuse Ezekiel from hearing and obeying - they just acknowledge the vast gulf between divine reality and creaturely perception.\
Marshwiggle:\
While that voice is mentioned in this chapter, the contents of that divine speech will need to wait for next chapter.  What we have here is the reaction, Ezekiel falling on his face.  On top of the overwhelming visual and auditory impact of the glory, we now get a concise description of the whole body impact.  And yet, as you say, the description of that glory is very much not the glory.\
That vast gulf you as you called it is not just one between divine reality and creaturely perception.  The expanse separates creator and creation.  Even the living creatures are on the creaturely side of that gulf.  Even they, perhaps especially they, respond to the holiness on the other side of that gulf by covering their bodies with a pair of wings.  Or, to put it the other way, the separation is an expression of that holiness.\
But the very word used to put extra distance between divine reality and creaturely perception is a sign of God reaching out to cross that gap.  Of course, without God having opened heaven and revealed these things, Ezekiel sees nothing.  But on top of that, the word 'likeness', for all it teaches us of that gap, also reaches back to its use in Genesis, when man was made.  Let us make man in our image, after our likeness.\
Opus:\
The 
\f1 \uc0\u1491 \u1456 \u1468 \u1502 \u1493 \u1468 \u1514 
\f0  that creates distance also recalls divine intention for proximity. Humanity made in God's likeness now sees God's glory through likeness - the mediating term becomes a reminder of original design. What separates also connects, but only because God initiates both the making and the revealing.\
This reframes the overwhelming nature of the vision. Ezekiel falls on his face not just from sensory overload but from recognizing simultaneously the intended connection (likeness) and the actual separation (layers of mediation). To see the appearance of the likeness of glory is to be reminded both of what humanity was made for and how far from that purpose we've fallen.\
The creatures' self-covering becomes more poignant in this light. Even beings who never fell, who exist in perfect obedience to the Spirit's movement, still recognize the need for reverent concealment before holiness. How much more the exiled priest, the son of Adam, in Babylon?\
The expanse as separator takes on new meaning too. Not arbitrary barrier but necessary protection - yet a protection that God's voice will traverse. The holiness that requires distance also compels communication. The God who cannot be looked upon insists on being heard.\
Marshwiggle:\
Heard, present, known to be present.  But that holiness, that presence, the formality of that presence, is also a warning, as is the fire with which that is revealed.  If God sees, if his glory is everywhere, if he can travel without limit - for alongside the mobility we have already discussed, the wheels drive home that message, then he might suddenly come - and what will happen to fallen humanity, to this fallen world when he does?\
Opus:\
The wheels within wheels, the eyes all around, the movement in any direction without turning - these multiply the mobility symbolism to an almost oppressive degree. Not just that God can go anywhere, but that divine oversight is already everywhere, ready to manifest at any moment. The fire that revealed becomes fire that judges.\
This vision in exile becomes especially sharp. The very people who thought themselves secure in having God's temple learned that divine presence can become divine absence as judgment. Now Ezekiel sees that same presence can burst forth in foreign territory - the God who left can arrive anywhere.\
The fallen world's encounter with sudden divine arrival is precisely what the layers of mediation both reveal and shield against. Every qualification - appearance, likeness - acknowledges that full divine presence would be consuming fire to corruption. Yet the vision's very existence proclaims that consumption isn't God's only option.\
"What will happen?" is the question the whole prophetic book will explore. Judgment, yes - but also the possibility of restoration. The wheels that can depart can also return. The fire that destroys can also purify. The glory that overwhelms the fallen priest by the Chebar still chooses to speak rather than simply consume.\
Marshwiggle:\
The rest of the book is for another conversation.  For now, I want to bring in the rest of the Bible.  Some Bible texts might implicitly reference Ezekiel 1, as an allusion, using what Ezekiel says as imagery for their own messages.  Other Bible texts might be illuminated, almost a reverse allusion.  That is, we ask what we might learn about that other text, given what Ezekiel 1 is teaching.\
With that procedure in mind, what do you think of Daniel 7 and Daniel 10?\
Opus:\
Daniel 7's throne vision directly incorporates Ezekiel's imagery while transforming it. The throne has wheels of burning fire, but now they're part of the throne itself rather than mysterious separate entities. The Ancient of Days sits without layers of linguistic mediation - Daniel sees more directly what Ezekiel saw through appearances of likenesses.\
But Daniel 7 adds the judicial element explicitly. Thrones (plural) are placed, books opened, beasts judged. Ezekiel's mobile throne becomes a courtroom. The fire that manifested presence now executes judgment. The overwhelming glory that made Ezekiel fall becomes the authority that strips kingdoms of power.\
Daniel 10 presents a different parallel - the overwhelming encounter with a divine/angelic figure by a river. Like Ezekiel by the Chebar, Daniel experiences bodily collapse. But where Ezekiel could hear immediately after falling, Daniel needs strengthening to even process communication. The progression suggests increasing intensity of divine encounter as history moves toward its climax.\
Most significantly, both Daniel passages show heavenly realities determining earthly politics. The beasts rise from the sea but are judged from the throne. The angelic figure in Daniel 10 speaks of cosmic warfare affecting earthly kingdoms. Ezekiel's vision of divine mobility anywhere becomes Daniel's revelation that divine sovereignty actively rules over all kingdoms - even when they seem to prevail.\
Marshwiggle:\
What about Psalm 104?\
Opus:\
Psalm 104 transforms Ezekiel's overwhelming vision into creation's normal operations. "He makes the clouds his chariot; he rides on the wings of the wind; he makes his messengers winds, his ministers a flame of fire" (vv. 3-4). What devastates Ezekiel as extraordinary revelation, the psalmist celebrates as God's regular way of moving through creation.\
This raises profound questions about perception. The psalmist sees wind and declares it God's messenger. Ezekiel sees creatures moved by wind/spirit and falls on his face. Same reality, different levels of unveiling? Or does the exile context make visible what comfortable worshippers in Jerusalem could sing about abstractly?\
The psalm's "ministers a flame of fire" (
\f1 \uc0\u1502 \u1456 \u1513 \u1464 \u1473 \u1512 \u1456 \u1514 \u1464 \u1497 \u1493 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1488 \u1461 \u1513 \u1473 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1500 \u1465 \u1492 \u1461 \u1496 
\f0 ) directly parallels Ezekiel's fiery phenomena. But where Ezekiel struggles to describe alien creatures emerging from fire, the psalmist simply states that fire serves God. The psalm domesticates divine presence into worship, while Ezekiel's vision shatters domestication.\
Most tellingly, Psalm 104:24 declares "O LORD, how manifold are your works!" - celebrating complexity where Ezekiel is overwhelmed by it. Perhaps the psalm shows what Ezekiel's vision should produce after recovery from the initial shock: not just terror but wonder at God's multifaceted presence throughout creation.\
Marshwiggle:\
What about Revelation chapters 4 and 5, or chapters 20 and 21?\
Opus:\
Revelation 4-5 synthesizes Ezekiel's vision with Isaiah 6, creating something new. The four living creatures retain their faces (lion, ox, human, eagle) but now cry "Holy, holy, holy" continuously. They're not beneath an expanse but around the throne itself - the separation has collapsed. What Ezekiel saw through multiple layers of mediation, John sees directly: "One seated on the throne."\
But Revelation 5 adds what Ezekiel couldn't see: the Lamb standing as though slain. The fire that should consume the fallen instead bears the marks of having been consumed himself. The throne of judgment becomes the throne of redemption.\
Revelation 20-21 completes the trajectory. Earth and sky flee from the One on the throne - creation itself cannot bear direct divine presence. But then comes the new creation where "the dwelling place of God is with man." The expanse that separated, the mediation that protected, the distance that holiness required - all gone.\
Most strikingly: "I saw no temple in the city, for its temple is the Lord God the Almighty and the Lamb" (21:22). The glory Ezekiel saw mediated through appearances of likenesses becomes the immediate environment of redeemed humanity. The protective barriers weren't permanent - they were temporary necessities until the cosmos could be made capable of enduring unmediated divine presence.\
Marshwiggle:\
What about John 3 then?\
Opus:\
John 3 addresses the very problem Ezekiel 1 poses: how can earthly beings comprehend heavenly realities? "If I have told you earthly things and you do not believe, how can you believe if I tell you heavenly things?" (v. 12). Ezekiel needed layers of mediation just to survive seeing the appearance of likeness of glory. Nicodemus can't even grasp earthly analogies for spiritual birth.\
But then comes the crucial claim: "No one has ascended into heaven except he who descended from heaven, the Son of Man" (v. 13). The expanse that separated heaven and earth in Ezekiel has been crossed - not by human ascent but by divine descent. The One on the throne has come down.\
Most profoundly, verse 14-15 transforms Ezekiel's imagery of judgment. The Son of Man must be "lifted up" like the bronze serpent. The throne of fire that threatened destruction becomes the cross where divine presence absorbs destruction. The overwhelming glory that made Ezekiel fall on his face enters the world in a form that draws rather than devastates.\
"God so loved the world" - not just Israel, not just the exiles by the Chebar, but the cosmos under judgment. The fire that Ezekiel saw ready to break forth anywhere now offers life rather than consumption to "whoever believes."\
