Opus:\
Acts 7 strikes me as most directly relevant. Stephen, about to be martyred, "gazed into heaven and saw the glory of God, and Jesus standing at the right hand of God" (v. 55). Like Ezekiel's opened heavens, but with crucial differences.\
The standing posture matters. Judges rise to pronounce verdict, advocates stand to defend. The One who sat on Ezekiel's throne-chariot now stands at God's right hand as Stephen faces death. The overwhelming glory that prostrated Ezekiel becomes the sustaining vision for the martyr.\
Most significantly, Stephen sees this while his accusers "ground their teeth at him" (v. 54). Like Ezekiel in hostile Babylon, divine glory appears precisely where human authority claims absolute power. The Sanhedrin thinks they control who lives and dies, but Stephen sees the true court in session.\
The vision's effect inverts Ezekiel's. Where Ezekiel fell on his face, overwhelmed, Stephen's face shines like an angel (6:15) and he prays for his killers. The same glory that demonstrated divine mobility and judgment in Ezekiel now reveals divine advocacy and mercy. The fire that threatened consumption enables Stephen to be consumed for witness rather than by judgment.\
