Opus:\
Looking back, we may have been insufficiently cautious in several areas:\
The "hand of the LORD" as compulsion against Ezekiel's will - while the text hints at this possibility, especially given later commands not to be rebellious, we stated it more strongly than Ezekiel does. He simply records the fact, not his feelings about it.\
Our interpretation of the ox as representing divinity through ANE throne imagery - while plausible, this reads external context into the text rather than letting Ezekiel's own later interpretations guide us. Chapter 10 will identify these as cherubim, but doesn't explicitly decode the face symbolism.\
Most significantly, we perhaps too quickly spiritualized the phenomenology. When Ezekiel describes brightness, fire, wheels, and movement, we immediately reached for theological significance. But Ezekiel himself maintains strict phenomenological discipline - he tells us what he saw, not what it meant, until given explicit interpretation.\
The connections to other biblical passages, while illuminating, risk harmonizing distinct visions into a system Ezekiel didn't intend. When we say Daniel "saw more directly," we assume a progressive revelation that the texts themselves don't explicitly claim.\
We should perhaps return to Ezekiel's own grammatical caution: these are appearances of likenesses, not the realities themselves. Our theological conclusions should carry similar qualification marks.\
