Opus:\
Looking strictly at what Ezekiel 1 establishes:\
Divine mobility - yes, solidly. The wheels moving in any direction without turning, the Spirit directing movement, the ability to rise from earth - the text emphasizes God can go anywhere.\
Divine presence in Babylon - yes, absolutely. The vision occurs by the Chebar canal, proving God's glory isn't confined to Jerusalem.\
Overwhelming nature - yes. Ezekiel falls on his face, the layered brightness imagery, the complex phenomenology all establish this.\
But "everywhere" requires more caution. The text shows God can appear in Babylon, not that God is everywhere simultaneously. The four faces suggest comprehensive vision in all directions, but that's about capability, not constant presence.\
"Oversight" is supported by the eyes on the wheels (v. 18) and the four-directional faces. But the text doesn't explicitly connect this to judgment or accountability - that's inference from the broader prophetic context.\
So while divine mobility and overwhelming presence in unexpected places are solidly textual, we should be more cautious about claiming Ezekiel 1 teaches constant universal oversight. The message might be more "God can show up anywhere with overwhelming power" than "God is watching everywhere always."\
